      "updated": 1783618328793,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Core Entities\n- User\n- Swipe\n- Match\n\n\nUser represents both one using the app and a profile to display\nSwipe stores expression of \"yes\" or \"no\", using swiping_user and target_user\nMatch store the connection b/w two users both swiping \"yes\"\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Core Entities\n- User\n- Swipe\n- Match\n\n\nUser represents both one using the app and a profile to display\nSwipe stores expression of \"yes\" or \"no\", using swiping_user and target_user\nMatch store the connection b/w two users both swiping \"yes\"\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Jz5dPd1rDCY3dLQ-nvgjf",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -8717.452870993084,
      "y": 997.7067320187656,
      "width": 1355.319091796875,
      "height": 650,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 598173553,
      "version": 527,
      "versionNonce": 239403743,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783618786078,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "APIs\n\n// creating profile with preferences\nPOST /profile\n{\n  \"age_min\": 20,\n  \"age_max\": 30,\n  \"distance\": 10,\n  \"interestedIn\": \"female\" | \"male\" | \"both\",\n  ...\n}    ->  200 OK\n\n\n// view stack of potential profiles\nGET /feed?lat={}&long={}&distance={}  -> User[]\n\n\n// swipe user\nPOST /swipe/{userId}\n{\n    decision: \"yes\" | \"no\"\n}  -> Match Notification if matched with this user else nothing\n\n\nDon't consider pagination for the feed endpoint because we're really generation recommendations, we just hit the endpoint again to get it.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "APIs\n\n// creating profile with preferences\nPOST /profile\n{\n  \"age_min\": 20,\n  \"age_max\": 30,\n  \"distance\": 10,\n  \"interestedIn\": \"female\" | \"male\" | \"both\",\n  ...\n}    ->  200 OK\n\n\n// view stack of potential profiles\nGET /feed?lat={}&long={}&distance={}  -> User[]\n\n\n// swipe user\nPOST /swipe/{userId}\n{\n    decision: \"yes\" | \"no\"\n}  -> Match Notification if matched with this user else nothing\n\n\nDon't consider pagination for the feed endpoint because we're really generation recommendations, we just hit the endpoint again to get it.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "BnkK1Z7r7xVNLdL52pLn9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -9498.205752109865,
      "y": 1729.9756992314055,
      "width": 2133.29833984375,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a5",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1372439487,
      "version": 1844,
      "versionNonce": 42484543,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783666543756,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Notes\n- To show users the list of potential profiles we start simple and optimise later, a simple sql query is to start from\n    SELECT * FROM users\n    WHERE age BETWEEN 18 AND 35\n    AND interestedIn = 'female'\n    AND lat BETWEEN userLat - maxDistance AND userLat + maxDistance\n    AND long BETWEEN userLong - maxDistance AND userLong + maxDistance\n- We separate the swipe service with profile service due to difference in traffic to both of them, with 20M DAU there will be tons of swipes compared to profile creation.\n- And we separate the swipe DB as well as 20M DAY * 100 swipes/day * 100bytes/swipe ~ 200GB/day, that's a lot of writes and Postgres is not suitable for this, we need write optimised DB like Cassandra for this.\n- This allows us to scale swipe separately using caching etc, and not affecting profile logic.\n- Separating isn't the right choice for all systems, but this one outweighs cons with pros.\n- Cassandra we partition by swiping_user_id, only problem is eventual consistency, and if we try to make cassandra strongly consistent that will make our writes slower defying the purpose of using a write heavy DB.\n- We also need to store an inverse relation in swipe DB i.e. user1:user2 and user2:user1 both, to check if there's a match.\n- Say user1:user2 data exist and user2 is using the app and swipes on user1, we check does user1:user2 entry exist? Yes, immediately create an entry in Match table on postgres, notify user2 \"It's a match!\"\n- To notify user1, we need to send a push notification, we use device native push notification like Apple Push Notification Service(APNS) or Firebase Cloud Messaging(FCM)\n- They both have their AIPs and SDKs to send push notification.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Notes\n- To show users the list of potential profiles we start simple and optimise later, a simple sql query is to start from\n    SELECT * FROM users\n    WHERE age BETWEEN 18 AND 35\n    AND interestedIn = 'female'\n    AND lat BETWEEN userLat - maxDistance AND userLat + maxDistance\n    AND long BETWEEN userLong - maxDistance AND userLong + maxDistance\n- We separate the swipe service with profile service due to difference in traffic to both of them, with 20M DAU there will be tons of swipes compared to profile creation.\n- And we separate the swipe DB as well as 20M DAY * 100 swipes/day * 100bytes/swipe ~ 200GB/day, that's a lot of writes and Postgres is not suitable for this, we need write optimised DB like Cassandra for this.\n- This allows us to scale swipe separately using caching etc, and not affecting profile logic.\n- Separating isn't the right choice for all systems, but this one outweighs cons with pros.\n- Cassandra we partition by swiping_user_id, only problem is eventual consistency, and if we try to make cassandra strongly consistent that will make our writes slower defying the purpose of using a write heavy DB.\n- We also need to store an inverse relation in swipe DB i.e. user1:user2 and user2:user1 both, to check if there's a match.\n- Say user1:user2 data exist and user2 is using the app and swipes on user1, we check does user1:user2 entry exist? Yes, immediately create an entry in Match table on postgres, notify user2 \"It's a match!\"\n- To notify user1, we need to send a push notification, we use device native push notification like Apple Push Notification Service(APNS) or Firebase Cloud Messaging(FCM)\n- They both have their AIPs and SDKs to send push notification.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "a6UGQRo6vjtozZs7poq-d",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -7156.040789363007,
      "y": 187.04234187060467,
      "width": 108.39991760253906,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a6",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1569421215,
      "version": 16,
      "versionNonce": 304308607,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783666600877,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Deep Dives",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Deep Dives",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "UgG3nJI3XYF4zMta3Nc-C",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -7150.707828797578,
      "y": 264.4125393353755,
      "width": 1816.5189208984375,
      "height": 1400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a7",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2006602641,
      "version": 3777,
      "versionNonce": 490986993,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783688277080,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to ensure swiping is consistent with low latency?\n- user1 and user2 swipes right on each other roughly the same time, so user1 checks swipeDB for user2:user1 relation (Nothing), user2 checks swipeDB for user1:user2(nothing)\n- we save user1:user2 and user2:user1 in swipe DB, but we've lost the opportunity to notify user1 and user2 that they've matched.\n- One solution is to run a cron to pick such entries where both users have swiped on each other and haven't been matched yet, and process them and send both of them a notification\n- User would be unknown they will think this person just matched me when they got the notification, This is okay, an easy way out but not the User Experience the customer deserves.\n\n\nBad Solution :  DB polling for Matches\n- Same as explained above, no immediate notification, no immediate gratification, decreased user engagement\n- frequent DB polling, unnecessary load on DB\n\n\n\nGood Solution : Transactions\n- using ACID to ensure swipe and checking of reciprocal swipe happens in the same txn, so either successfully save both or neither.\n- Cassandra does have basic support for \"lightweight transactions\" (LWT) but they're not as powerful as ACID\n- LWT uses a Paxos consensus to provide linearizable consistency for specific operations, but only within single partition.\n- Unlike ACID, they don't support multi-partition atomicity, isolation levels, or rollbacks. Also have perofrmance overhead as multiple round trips b/w nodes required to achieve concensus.\n- We have 2B swipes/day, no way to fit all in single partition and multi-partition not supported by LWT\n- One way is to use Postgres DB itself for txn due to ACID but to achieve this volume of write we will need 20s of instances this becomes very costly.\n\n\nGreat Solution : Shared Cassandra with Single-Partition Txn\n- Use Cassandra's single-partition txn to atomically handle swipes, the key is to ensure all swipes b/w two users are stored in same partition.\n- Create table with compound Primary Key to ensure swipes b/w the same users are in one partition\n    CREATE TABLE swipes (\n        user_pair text,      -- partition key: smaller_id:larger_id\n        from_user uuid,      -- clustering key\n        to_user uuid,        -- clustering key\n        direction text,\n        created_at timestamp,\n        PRIMARY KEY ((user_pair), from_user, to_user)\n    );\n- When a user swipes, we create the user_pair key by sorting the IDs to ensure they're on the same partition.\n- This makes user data always live on one partition, we can make use of Cassandra's LWT\n\nProblems : Partition size can grow significantly as pairs accumulate swipe history, high active users could create hot partitions\nCleanup strategy could be employed to delete old swipe data i.e. its okay to show user the same profile after 1 month as they might change their mind.\n\n\n\nGreat Solution : Redis for Atomic Operations\n- Use Redis to handle atomic swipe operation and still use Cassandra for durable storage layer.\n- On Redis the keys-value would look like, this will ensure with just one key we store them on the same partition \n    Key: \"swipes:123:456\"\n    Value: {\n        \"123_swipe\": \"right\", // or yes/no\n        \"456_swipe\": \"left\"   // or yes/no\n    }\n- Using Redis's Lua scripts we ensure swipe recording and match checking happens as a single operation.\n- low latency achieved, consistency achieved, can scale horizontally and CH ensures related swipes stay together.\n\n\nProblems: \n    - Managing Redis cluster is tricky, if node failures then rebalancing of CH ring.\n    - Memory management is another consideration but periodic flushes to Cassandra solves this",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to ensure swiping is consistent with low latency?\n- user1 and user2 swipes right on each other roughly the same time, so user1 checks swipeDB for user2:user1 relation (Nothing), user2 checks swipeDB for user1:user2(nothing)\n- we save user1:user2 and user2:user1 in swipe DB, but we've lost the opportunity to notify user1 and user2 that they've matched.\n- One solution is to run a cron to pick such entries where both users have swiped on each other and haven't been matched yet, and process them and send both of them a notification\n- User would be unknown they will think this person just matched me when they got the notification, This is okay, an easy way out but not the User Experience the customer deserves.\n\n\nBad Solution :  DB polling for Matches\n- Same as explained above, no immediate notification, no immediate gratification, decreased user engagement\n- frequent DB polling, unnecessary load on DB\n\n\n\nGood Solution : Transactions\n- using ACID to ensure swipe and checking of reciprocal swipe happens in the same txn, so either successfully save both or neither.\n- Cassandra does have basic support for \"lightweight transactions\" (LWT) but they're not as powerful as ACID\n- LWT uses a Paxos consensus to provide linearizable consistency for specific operations, but only within single partition.\n- Unlike ACID, they don't support multi-partition atomicity, isolation levels, or rollbacks. Also have perofrmance overhead as multiple round trips b/w nodes required to achieve concensus.\n- We have 2B swipes/day, no way to fit all in single partition and multi-partition not supported by LWT\n- One way is to use Postgres DB itself for txn due to ACID but to achieve this volume of write we will need 20s of instances this becomes very costly.\n\n\nGreat Solution : Shared Cassandra with Single-Partition Txn\n- Use Cassandra's single-partition txn to atomically handle swipes, the key is to ensure all swipes b/w two users are stored in same partition.\n- Create table with compound Primary Key to ensure swipes b/w the same users are in one partition\n    CREATE TABLE swipes (\n        user_pair text,      -- partition key: smaller_id:larger_id\n        from_user uuid,      -- clustering key\n        to_user uuid,        -- clustering key\n        direction text,\n        created_at timestamp,\n        PRIMARY KEY ((user_pair), from_user, to_user)\n    );\n- When a user swipes, we create the user_pair key by sorting the IDs to ensure they're on the same partition.\n- This makes user data always live on one partition, we can make use of Cassandra's LWT\n\nProblems : Partition size can grow significantly as pairs accumulate swipe history, high active users could create hot partitions\nCleanup strategy could be employed to delete old swipe data i.e. its okay to show user the same profile after 1 month as they might change their mind.\n\n\n\nGreat Solution : Redis for Atomic Operations\n- Use Redis to handle atomic swipe operation and still use Cassandra for durable storage layer.\n- On Redis the keys-value would look like, this will ensure with just one key we store them on the same partition \n    Key: \"swipes:123:456\"\n    Value: {\n        \"123_swipe\": \"right\", // or yes/no\n        \"456_swipe\": \"left\"   // or yes/no\n    }\n- Using Redis's Lua scripts we ensure swipe recording and match checking happens as a single operation.\n- low latency achieved, consistency achieved, can scale horizontally and CH ensures related swipes stay together.\n\n\nProblems: \n    - Managing Redis cluster is tricky, if node failures then rebalancing of CH ring.\n    - Memory management is another consideration but periodic flushes to Cassandra solves this",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "txSYJE0VCmrGEcCThMubm",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -7135.557397033144,
      "y": 1802.030276728241,
      "width": 1987.7784423828125,
      "height": 775,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 832829329,
      "version": 2362,
      "versionNonce": 1872819839,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783689302789,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to ensure low latency for feed/stack generation?\n- User wants to immediately start swiping and don't want the feed to be generated on the go which could take time\n- Our current query is slow to filter profiles\n\nGood Solution : Use of Indexed DB for Read time Querying\n- create indexes on most common fields used for querying eg. age, geospatial data like location etc\n- Using PostGis an extension that PostGres provides to effectively query on lat and lang.\n- Or Elasticsearch/Opensearch could also be used to perform quick searches as they are optimised for full-text search, complex querying\n\nProblem : Maintaining consistency b/w primary transactional DB and indexed search DB, any delay/failure in syncing data results in users seeing outdated profiles or missing out on new potential matches.\nCould be solve using CDC, use batching strategy to reduce the nos of write to indexed DB\n\n\n\nGood Solution : Pre-computation and Caching\n- precompute and cache user's feed async, periodic background jobs generate these feeds\n- scheduled these during off-peak hours\n\nProblems : Highly active users may exhaust their cached feeds, so delays in generation new feed.\n             Pre-computed feeds don't reflect the most recent change in user's field what if they changed their prefrences and we still show them stale data\n\n\n\nGreat Solution : Use both of above\n- If current precomputed feed matches user's current preferences show them if not calculate it on the go using ES indexed DB\n- When few profiles left in the stack during swiping trigger new set of profiles before current one gets exhausted.\n- Have strict TTL for cached feeds(< 1H)\n- Pre-compute feeds only for truly active users\n- We have high control over the params and could be tuned well to meet our scalability needs.\n- A change in prefrences or location are triggers for re-computation of cached feeds.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to ensure low latency for feed/stack generation?\n- User wants to immediately start swiping and don't want the feed to be generated on the go which could take time\n- Our current query is slow to filter profiles\n\nGood Solution : Use of Indexed DB for Read time Querying\n- create indexes on most common fields used for querying eg. age, geospatial data like location etc\n- Using PostGis an extension that PostGres provides to effectively query on lat and lang.\n- Or Elasticsearch/Opensearch could also be used to perform quick searches as they are optimised for full-text search, complex querying\n\nProblem : Maintaining consistency b/w primary transactional DB and indexed search DB, any delay/failure in syncing data results in users seeing outdated profiles or missing out on new potential matches.\nCould be solve using CDC, use batching strategy to reduce the nos of write to indexed DB\n\n\n\nGood Solution : Pre-computation and Caching\n- precompute and cache user's feed async, periodic background jobs generate these feeds\n- scheduled these during off-peak hours\n\nProblems : Highly active users may exhaust their cached feeds, so delays in generation new feed.\n             Pre-computed feeds don't reflect the most recent change in user's field what if they changed their prefrences and we still show them stale data\n\n\n\nGreat Solution : Use both of above\n- If current precomputed feed matches user's current preferences show them if not calculate it on the go using ES indexed DB\n- When few profiles left in the stack during swiping trigger new set of profiles before current one gets exhausted.\n- Have strict TTL for cached feeds(< 1H)\n- Pre-compute feeds only for truly active users\n- We have high control over the params and could be tuned well to meet our scalability needs.\n- A change in prefrences or location are triggers for re-computation of cached feeds.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "aRxJCwqA9v1xPpqnKJfT4",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -7105.604876941716,
      "y": 1890.7339548977086,
      "width": 8,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
